      "Rastafari has no canonical scripture beyond the Bible, which is read selectively and interpreted through Afrocentric lens. The **King James Bible** is most commonly used, though Rastafari critique it as Babylon-corrupted, arguing that white translators changed names (Jesus should be 'Yesus' or 'Iyesus'; God is 'Jah,' shortened from Jehovah; Israelites were Africans), suppressed African passages, and inserted pro-slavery, pro-white content. Some Rastafari prefer the **Ethiopian Bible** or **Amharic scriptures** to access less-corrupted versions, though few speak Amharic. Key biblical books for Rastafari include: **Psalms** (especially 68, 87, 137—Ethiopianist and exilic themes); **Revelation** (5:5, 19:16—Messiah as Lion of Judah, King of Kings); **Genesis** (African origin of humanity); **Exodus** (liberation from oppression); **Daniel** (prophetic visions); and **Leviticus/Numbers** (dietary laws, Nazarite vow). New Testament is read cautiously—Rastafari honor Jesus ('Yesus Kristos') as prophet or manifestation of Jah, but Haile Selassie is the fulfillment, the returned Christ.",
